<section id="s-bottleneck" data-code="bottleneck">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Byte Bottleneck</div>
<h2>Why byte-level models are slower than they should be</h2>
<p>Byte-level language models are appealing: no tokenizer means no sensitivity to spelling noise, no multilingual disparities, and direct character-level understanding. The <strong>Byte Latent Transformer (BLT)</strong> already narrows the efficiency gap by grouping bytes into variable-length <em>patches</em> using entropy-based segmentation — complex text gets short patches (more global-model calls), predictable text gets long patches (fewer calls). Yet BLT still generates output <em>one byte at a time</em>.</p>
<p>The problem is memory bandwidth. Modern LLM inference is dominated not by arithmetic but by loading model weights from DRAM into compute units. Every decoder forward pass loads the full weight matrix. BLT's decoder outputs one byte and stops; the encoder and global model must then re-run to start the next patch. A typical subword token spans ~4 bytes, so BLT's decoder is called roughly 4× more often than a BPE model's decoder for the same text — even after patching reduces calls elsewhere.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">decoder-calls-comparison.svg</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Generating "_BLT_Won" (8 bytes) — click Animate</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="gen-canvas" class="gen-canvas" height="220"></canvas>
    <div class="ctrl-row">
      <button class="ctrl-btn" id="gen-animate-btn">▶ Animate</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" id="gen-reset-btn">↺ Reset</button>
      <span class="ctrl-label" id="gen-status">Click Animate to compare generation strategies</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The canvas above shows the contrast head-on: generating eight bytes of text <code>_BLT_Won</code> (the example from Figure 1 of the paper). Standard BLT needs eight separate decoder forward passes — one per byte, shown as sequential amber rows. BLT-D groups those same eight bytes into a <em>block</em> and generates them in just two parallel steps, shown in green. Both produce identical text; BLT-D uses 75% fewer decoder calls.</p>
<p>The goal of this paper is to make byte-level models viable for production inference by reducing both decoder NFEs (network function evaluations) and the resulting memory bandwidth pressure.</p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s-diffusion" data-code="diffusion">
<div class="section-no">02 — Block Diffusion Decoding</div>
<h2>Generating an entire byte block in parallel</h2>
<p>BLT-D's key idea borrows from <strong>discrete diffusion language models</strong>. Instead of autoregressively predicting byte <em>i+1</em> given all bytes up to <em>i</em>, the decoder receives a block of <strong>[MASK]</strong> tokens and iteratively unmasks them in parallel — the same approach as MDLM and SEDD, adapted for BLT's hierarchical architecture.</p>
<p>At inference time, the encoder and global model run exactly as in standard BLT, producing a set of latent token representations. The local decoder then processes a fixed-size block <em>B</em> of masked positions after the clean prefix, using <strong>bidirectional attention within the block</strong> (so every masked position can attend to every other). On each decoder step, the model scores the full vocabulary for each masked position and unmasks the most confident ones. This repeats until the block is full.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">block-diffusion-sim.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Choose block size B, then click Run Diffusion</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="mask-canvas" class="mask-canvas" height="200"></canvas>
    <div class="ctrl-row">
      <span class="ctrl-label">Block size B =</span>
      <button class="ctrl-btn active" id="b-btn-4" data-b="4">4</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" id="b-btn-8" data-b="8">8</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" id="b-btn-16" data-b="16">16</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" id="mask-run-btn">▶ Run Diffusion</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" id="mask-reset-btn">↺ Reset</button>
      <span class="ctrl-label" id="mask-status">Step 0 / ? — all masked</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The simulator above shows the unmasking process for the phrase <code>_BLT_Won_it!</code>. Each step, the most-confident masked positions are revealed simultaneously. With <strong>B=4</strong> you typically need 2 steps to unmask a 4-byte block; with <strong>B=8</strong> about 3 steps; with <strong>B=16</strong> about 4–5 steps. The key point: <em>s &lt; B</em> — you always save decoder calls compared to byte-by-byte generation.</p>

<h3>Two unmasking strategies</h3>
<p>The paper explores two ways to select which positions to unmask at each step:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Confidence-based:</strong> unmask all positions whose max predicted probability exceeds a threshold α. Simple and fast; works well with greedy decoding.</li>
  <li><strong>Entropy-bounded (EB) sampling:</strong> rank positions by entropy, select the largest subset whose cumulative entropy stays under threshold γ. Enables top-p sampling for diverse outputs.</li>
</ul>
<p>Both strategies guarantee progress — if no position meets the threshold, the single most-confident position is unmasked anyway. This ensures the loop always terminates.</p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s-training" data-code="training">
<div class="section-no">03 — Training BLT-D</div>
<h2>A dual objective: autoregressive + masked reconstruction</h2>
<p>The core challenge: BLT-D must generate future bytes <em>beyond the current patch boundary</em> during inference. Standard BLT's decoder is only trained to predict within the current patch. BLT-D trains the same decoder with both objectives simultaneously.</p>

<h3>Training data preprocessing</h3>
<p>For each training sequence, the entropy patcher segments it into variable-length patches as usual (this becomes the <em>clean input</em>). A second <em>corrupted input</em> is constructed: for each patch boundary (except the first), a fixed-length block of <em>B</em> consecutive bytes starting at that boundary is extracted and independently masked with probability <em>t ~ Uniform(0,1)</em>. Because B &gt; average patch size, many blocks extend into the <em>next</em> patch — forcing the decoder to learn to predict ahead.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">blt-d-architecture.svg</span>
