<p>The decoder sees <em>both</em> inputs at once: the clean prefix (causal attention, left side of the SVG) and the corrupted block (bidirectional attention within each block, right side). Cross-attention links both to the global model's latent tokens. This dual view trains the decoder to do two things: predict the next byte autoregressively (for BLT-compatibility), and reconstruct masked bytes in any order (for diffusion inference).</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Combined Training Loss</div>
  <span class="math-var">L_total</span> = <span class="math-var">L_clean</span> + <span class="math-var">L_mask</span>
  <br><br>
  <span class="math-var">L_clean</span> = &minus;&sum; log p(x<sub>i</sub> | x<sub>&lt;i</sub>) &nbsp;<span class="math-comment">// standard next-byte autoregressive loss</span>
  <br>
  <span class="math-var">L_mask</span> = &minus;(1/<span class="math-var">t</span>) &sum; <b>1</b>[x<sub>k</sub>=[MASK]] log p(x<sub>k</sub> | x<sup>t</sup><sub>block</sub>, x<sub>&lt;s_k</sub>) &nbsp;<span class="math-comment">// masked reconstruction, scaled by 1/t</span>
</div>

<p>The 1/<em>t</em> scaling is the standard absorbing-diffusion weighting: at low noise (small <em>t</em>), few positions are masked, so each masked position contributes more to the loss. This matches the ELBO derivation from masked diffusion theory.</p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s-speculation" data-code="speculation">
<div class="section-no">04 — Speculative Extensions</div>
<h2>BLT-S and BLT-DV: trading some speed for guaranteed quality</h2>
<p>Pure diffusion generation (BLT-D) is fast but can lose quality at large block sizes — the decoder must predict far into the future without fully autoregressive conditioning. Two speculative-decoding–inspired extensions address this:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>BLT-S (Self-speculation)</strong>: Standard BLT's decoder speculates beyond normal patch boundaries, then the full model verifies the draft. No new architecture, no training changes — just a different inference loop.</li>
  <li><strong>BLT-DV (Diffusion+Verification)</strong>: BLT-D's diffusion decoder proposes a full block, then an autoregressive forward pass verifies byte-by-byte. The same model parameters serve both roles, since BLT-D was trained with both objectives.</li>
</ul>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">speculation-walkthrough.steps</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Step through the BLT-S verification procedure</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="spec-steps">
      <div class="step-box active" id="step-0">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Decoder drafts k bytes beyond patch boundary</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Standard BLT would stop and call the encoder+global model when entropy spikes. Instead, BLT-S lets the decoder continue autoregressively for up to k more bytes, conditioned on the last available latent token.</div>
          <div class="step-code">prefix: "…_BLT"  →  draft: "_W", "o", "n", " " (k=4)</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="step-1">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Full model re-encodes the draft candidate sequence</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The encoder and global model process the entire sequence including the speculated bytes, producing fresh latent tokens. The decoder then runs with causal attention to produce next-byte predictions for each position.</div>
          <div class="step-code">E(x + draft) → T′ → G(T′) → O′ → D(x+draft; O′) → y</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="step-2">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Accept bytes up to first mismatch</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Compare drafted bytes to verified next-byte predictions, one by one. Accept all matching bytes. At the first mismatch, replace the drafted byte with the verified prediction and stop. This is an exact-match criterion (greedy decoding).</div>
          <div class="step-code">draft:    "_W" "o" "n" " "   ✓ ✓ ✓ ✗
verified:  "_W" "o" "n" "i"  → accept 3, use "i" for pos 4</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" id="step-3">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Continue from accepted position</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The loop advances by at least one byte per iteration (the verified replacement byte is always accepted). In the best case, all k bytes match and you get k+1 bytes for one encoder/global call — the "free byte" from next-byte prediction is a bonus.</div>
          <div class="step-code">accepted: "_Won"  →  resume from pos |prefix|+4</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="step-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
      <span id="step-prog" class="ctrl-label">Step 1 of 4</span>
      <button id="step-next">Next →</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>BLT-S reduces encoder/global NFEs without increasing them — you call the full model once per draft window instead of once per patch. With draft window k=16 and average patch size 4, that's a 4× reduction in expensive calls. BLT-DV works similarly: diffusion proposes the block, autoregressive verification accepts the good prefix, and the loop continues. Empirically, one-step diffusion (unmask everything at once) followed by verification gives the best speed-quality balance for BLT-DV.</p>
<p>A key advantage of both methods: <strong>no new parameters and no extra training</strong>. BLT-S exploits BLT's existing architecture; BLT-DV exploits the fact that BLT-D was already trained with the autoregressive objective.</p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s-tradeoff" data-code="tradeoff">
<div class="section-no">05 — The Speed–Quality Knob</div>
<h2>Block size B is the dial between fastest and most accurate</h2>
<p>BLT-D is pre-trained at three block sizes — <strong>B=4, 8, 16</strong> — giving you three distinct operating points on the speed-quality Pareto frontier. Larger B means more bytes in each block, fewer decoder and encoder/global calls per sequence, but the model must predict farther ahead with less autoregressive context.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">speed-quality-pareto.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">Memory bandwidth reduction vs ARC-Easy accuracy (3B models)</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="tradeoff-canvas" class="tradeoff-canvas" height="320"></canvas>
    <div class="ctrl-row">
      <span class="ctrl-label">Highlight:</span>
      <button class="ctrl-btn active" id="hl-all">All methods</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" id="hl-bltd">BLT-D family</button>
      <button class="ctrl-btn" id="hl-verify">With verification</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Several patterns jump out from the Pareto chart:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>BLT-S (k=16)</strong> sits near the top-right: 77% bandwidth reduction with essentially no ARC-Easy loss. It never trades quality, only speed.</li>
  <li><strong>BLT-D-4</strong> is nearly as fast as BLT-S with very similar quality — a compelling default.</li>
  <li><strong>BLT-D-16</strong> reaches 87–92% bandwidth reduction at the cost of ~7 ARC-Easy points. Worth it for translation; less so for code.</li>
  <li><strong>BLT-DV</strong> (verification on top of BLT-D-8/16) recovers most of the quality loss while maintaining substantial speedup vs BLT baseline.</li>
</ul>

<table class="results-table">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Method</th><th>BW Reduction</th><th>ARC-Easy</th><th>PIQA</th><th>HellaSwag</th><th>MMLU</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>BLT 3B (baseline)</td><td class="mn">0%</td><td class="mn best">74.33</td><td class="mn best">79.38</td><td class="mn best">74.98</td><td class="mn best">41.15</td></tr>
    <tr class="highlight-row"><td>BLT-D-4 3B</td><td class="mn">~52%</td><td class="mn">72.39</td><td class="mn">79.60</td><td class="mn">71.86</td><td class="mn">39.07</td></tr>
    <tr><td>BLT-D-8 3B</td><td class="mn">~70%</td><td class="mn">70.95</td><td class="mn">78.02</td><td class="mn">70.56</td><td class="mn">38.29</td></tr>
    <tr><td>BLT-D-16 3B</td><td class="mn">87–92%</td><td class="mn">66.89</td><td class="mn">76.93</td><td class="mn">69.12</td><td class="mn">37.08</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Note that PIQA is the odd one out: BLT-D-4 actually <em>improves</em> on BLT (79.60 vs 79.38). The paper attributes this to the regularization effect of the diffusion objective — the decoder becomes slightly more robust. The degradation is most visible on HellaSwag and especially on code generation tasks (HumanEval, MBPP), where long-range coherence within a block matters more.</p>
</section>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Benchmark Results</div>
<h2>How much faster, and at what cost?</h2>
<p>Across all evaluated tasks, the three methods form a spectrum:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>BLT-D</strong> is the fastest, with the largest bandwidth reduction. Quality holds well at B=4 but degrades at B=16 on code tasks.</li>
  <li><strong>BLT-DV</strong> adds a verification pass after diffusion drafting. It reduces bandwidth less than pure BLT-D but recovers much of the quality loss — particularly useful when code correctness matters.</li>
  <li><strong>BLT-S</strong> preserves exact BLT output quality (same decoding, guaranteed) with 77% fewer total model calls. It's strictly better than BLT on efficiency, with no quality cost.</li>
</ul>

