<p>The paper's memory bandwidth metric (Eq. 8) counts parameter loads across all model components weighted by their call counts. It's a hardware-agnostic proxy that isolates algorithmic efficiency from implementation details like kernel fusion or KV-cache management. Actual wall-clock speedups in optimized inference would vary, but the NFE reductions are reproducible guarantees.</p>
<p>Looking forward: the paper notes that a larger decoder would amplify BLT-D/BLT-DV's gains further, since those methods specifically reduce decoder NFEs. BLT-S benefits more from a lightweight decoder (extra decoder calls are cheap when the decoder is small). Both directions — and scaling to more training compute — remain open research questions.</p>
</section>

